<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Ma Chu</h1><p class="st-meta">907 CE – 951 CE · capital Changsha</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The kingdom of the Ma family in Hunan, which grew rich on the tea trade with northern China before collapsing in a family war in 951.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-yin">Ma Yin</a>, a carpenter turned soldier from Xuzhou, came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a> with a band of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and made himself master of the region from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changsha">Changsha</a> in 896. In 907 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-liang">Later Liang</a> recognised him as Prince of Chu, a title he held while accepting the overlordship of the northern courts.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-yin">Ma Yin</a> encouraged farming and especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, which was sold in large quantities to the northern dynasties in exchange for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and war horses. He issued iron and lead coins that could not be spent outside Chu, keeping goods and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> inside the kingdom. The ruling family relied on loyal generals and on alliances with the southern Han regimes.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-yin">Ma Yin</a> died in 930, his many sons fought over the throne. During a civil war between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-xiguang">Ma Xiguang</a> and Ma Xi'e, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/southern-tang">Southern Tang</a> invaded and in 951 took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changsha">Changsha</a>, ending the dynasty. Local commanders soon expelled the Southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/later-tang">Tang</a> and held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a> until the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> took it in 963.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ma-chu.jpg" alt="Ma Chu" width="320" height="437"><figcaption>Image: Pavo Xie · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:L.LIANG.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>907 CE – 951 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Changsha</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ma Yin, Ma Xisheng, Ma Xifan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mahayana Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chu (Ten Kingdoms), Southern Chu</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=929&amp;focus=ma-chu" aria-label="Ma Chu on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7639 1145 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ma Chu lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7959 1401L7855 1179M7959 1401L8056 1267"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7855 1179h0M8056 1267h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7959 1401h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7959 1401h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Changsha. <a href="/atlas?year=929&amp;focus=ma-chu">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

